          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-788df1cbe03c9c66\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted words occur within Loredana’s categorical negative declaration, “io non vi amerò mai” (“I shall never love you”). Its core force is truth-conditional: she presents her present disposition and future emotional relation to Berto as one in which love is impossible. Although the declaration decisively rejects him, it does not substantially undertake a commitment in the narrow P sense. Its vehemence is fully representable as contextual intensity attached to T and supplies no independent evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future tense could weakly suggest a resolution as well as a prediction, but the surrounding avowal that she loves Filippo and hates Berto strongly favors a categorical assertion of non-love rather than a promise or undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Loredana directly addresses Berto in narrated dialogue; the highlighted affirmative substring “vi amerò” is syntactically governed by “non ... mai,” yielding “I shall never love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Loredana uses the declaration to dispel Berto’s hopes and explain that her contemplated relationship with him would be a self-destructive sacrifice for Filippo, whom she says she truly loves. Her contempt, hatred, and savage vehemence intensify the rejection but do not change its core truth-conditional force. The narration subsequently contrasts her denial with Berto’s sincere avowal of love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Come devo dirvi che io non vi amerò mai?” (“How must I tell you that I shall never love you?”)\",\"supports\":\"The complete clause is an explicit, categorical assertion of enduring non-love toward Berto, supporting high T and clarifying that the highlighted substring is negated.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Loredana says, “Io amo Filippo” and explains that becoming Berto’s mistress would be her gravest sacrifice for Filippo.\",\"supports\":\"Her contrasting love for Filippo frames the target as a serious avowal about her emotional state rather than a conventional token or semantically empty phrase.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand she tells Berto, “non capite che io vi odio, e più vi avvicinate a me e più vi odio.”\",\"supports\":\"Her explicit hatred of Berto reinforces the propositional content of permanent non-love and weighs against interpreting the target primarily as an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration characterizes her speech as having “veemenza selvaggia” and as driven by a need “di mordere e di distruggere.”\",\"supports\":\"The delivery is intensely affective, but the text does not depict the words as an unpremeditated blurt or reflexive discharge; intensity alone therefore does not support E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a future-tense Italian love construction near the end of Luciano Zùccoli’s L’amore di Loredana.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical future form and bibliographic location, while the local clause supplies the crucial negation omitted from the exact-match substring.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the categorical presentation of future non-love. The rejection, hostility, and emotional vehemence are contextual functions and delivery features rather than residual aspects of core force requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied form metadata labels the exact-match substring affirmative, but the source clause is unambiguously negative because “vi amerò” is enclosed by “non ... mai.”\"}"
        }
      ],
